---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site, formatDate } from '../site';

const lastReviewed = new Date('2026-09-11');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Accessibility statement"
  description="How accessible this site is, how it was tested, what currently falls short, and how to report a barrier."
>
  <div class="page region">
    <p class="eyebrow">Accessibility statement</p>
    <h1>Accessibility of this site</h1>

    <div class="prose statement">
      <p class="statement__lede">
        This statement applies to {site.url}. It says what this site aims for,
        how it was tested, and what it currently gets wrong. Most statements
        skip that last part; it is the only part that tells you anything.
      </p>

      <h2>Conformance status</h2>
      <p>
        This site aims to conform to <strong>{site.wcagTarget}</strong>. It is
        currently <strong>partially conformant</strong>: most of the standard
        is met, and the known exceptions are listed below.
      </p>
      <p>
        &ldquo;Partially conformant&rdquo; is the honest answer for almost
        every site in active development, including this one. A claim of full
        conformance usually means nobody has looked hard enough.
      </p>

      <h2>How this site was tested</h2>
      <p>Testing is both automated and manual, because neither is sufficient alone.</p>
      <h3>On every pull request</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>axe-core, run through Playwright against every page of the built site</li>
        <li>HTML validation of the built output</li>
        <li>A failure in either blocks the merge</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Manually, before each release</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Keyboard-only navigation of every page and every interactive control</li>
        <li>VoiceOver on macOS and iOS</li>
        <li>NVDA on Windows <em>(placeholder — confirm or remove)</em></li>
        <li>400% browser zoom and a 320px viewport</li>
        <li>Windows High Contrast / forced-colors mode</li>
        <li>Reduced-motion preference enabled</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Automated tools catch roughly a third of WCAG issues.</strong>
        A clean axe run is a floor, not a conformance claim, and this
        statement does not treat it as one.
      </p>

      <h2>Known issues</h2>
      <p>
        <em>
          Placeholder — replace with real findings once the site is built and
          manually tested. Write them the way you would want a vendor to write
          them to you: what fails, which criterion, who it affects, and when
          it will be fixed.
        </em>
      </p>
      <!--
        A native <section> with an accessible name is exposed as a region,
        so no explicit role is needed. tabindex="0" makes the horizontal
        scroll reachable by keyboard — SC 1.4.10 with SC 2.1.1.
      -->
      <section class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="Known accessibility issues">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Issue</th>
              <th scope="col">Criterion</th>
              <th scope="col">Status</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Example — code blocks in posts have not been verified in forced-colors mode</th>
              <td>1.4.3, 1.4.11</td>
              <td>Open, fix expected this quarter</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Example — long code samples scroll horizontally without a visible affordance</th>
              <td>1.4.10</td>
              <td>Open, under review</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <h2>Technical specifications</h2>
      <p>
        This site relies on HTML, CSS and a small amount of JavaScript. All
        content is readable and all navigation works with JavaScript disabled.
        The contact form works without JavaScript, and validates in place when
        it is available.
      </p>

      <h2>Feedback</h2>
      <p>
        If anything here stopped you — a control you could not reach, text you
        could not read, a form you could not complete — please tell me. I
        treat it as a bug report, not a complaint.
      </p>
      <p>
        Email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> or use
        <a href="/contact/">the contact form</a>. I aim to respond within two
        working days and to tell you either when it will be fixed or why it
        will not be.
      </p>

      <h2>Preparation</h2>
      <p>
        This statement was prepared by self-assessment on
        <time datetime="2026-09-11">{formatDate(lastReviewed)}</time> and is
        reviewed whenever the site changes materially.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .statement__lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .statement h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
  }

  .statement h3 {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .statement .table-scroll {
    margin-block: var(--space-s);
  }
</style>
